  "narrative": {
    "essence": "Team Melli carries the weight of a nation — and in 2022, that weight became almost unbearable. Iran's players stood at the intersection of sport and political protest, facing impossible choices as the Woman, Life, Freedom movement swept through their homeland. 2026 brings them back to football's biggest stage still under that shadow.",
    "footballIdentity": "Football is Iran's most popular sport by a vast margin, with a passionate fanbase that fills the Azadi Stadium — one of the world's largest — for every home match. The Persian Gulf Pro League is competitive by regional standards, and Iran has been the dominant force in Asian football for decades, making six World Cup appearances. The national team is a source of enormous pride, even as the relationship between the state and football remains politically charged.",
    "theMoment": "In 2022, some Iranian players refused to sing the national anthem in solidarity with protesters back home, while others sang — the split visible to the entire world. That moment hasn't been forgotten by anyone. In 2026, Iran must navigate those same tensions while trying to actually win football matches. Getting out of Group G — past Belgium — would be the greatest achievement in Iranian football history.",
    "momentType": "political"
  },
